title: Facade builds Net::Async::HTTP without a timeout (and with redirects on)
updated: 2026-10-25T01:13:38Z
---

`lib/Net/Async/Zitadel.pm:47-49` creates the shared client as `Net::Async::HTTP->new( user_agent => ... )` and nothing else. Net::Async::HTTP applies no timeout unless one is given ("if not supplied then no default will be applied, and no timeout will take place"), so a Zitadel that accepts the connection and never answers leaves every `_f` Future pending for ever - discovery, JWKS and with them every `verify_token_f` that coalesced onto the in-flight fetch. The sync twin has 30 s (`WWW::Zitadel`, `LWP::UserAgent->new(timeout => 30)`); the Authentik and Keycloak twins pass `timeout => 30, max_redirects => 0, fail_on_error => 0` (`p5-net-async-keycloak/lib/Net/Async/Keycloak.pm:106`).

Second half, not reproduced: `max_redirects` is left at Net::Async::HTTP's default of 3, so a redirect answer to a Management call is followed with the same request, including the `Authorization: Bearer <PAT>` header. The siblings switch redirects off for exactly that reason.

Done: the default client is built with `timeout => 30` (or `stall_timeout`), `max_redirects => 0`; POD of `http` says what the default is; a test asserts both settings on the built object.

